The enforcement point also has a classic name. In October 1972 a planning study for the US Air Force led by James P. Anderson, a computer security consultant, introduced the **reference monitor**: something that "enforces the authorized access relationships between subjects and objects". A **subject** is an active thing that asks (a user or a process); an **object** is what is asked for (a file, a record, an API). TCSEC quotes the three requirements the Anderson report set for any implementation of it:

- it must be **tamper proof**;
- it must **always be invoked**;
- it must be **small enough to be subject to analysis and tests**, the completeness of which can be assured.

You met these as design principles already: always invoked is complete mediation, small is economy of mechanism. The rest of this course keeps finding reference monitors under new names: an authorization middleware, a policy enforcement point, an identity-aware proxy.

